{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggkmdtkEwrofisSy3YU2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**The spin crossover happens at Dq ≈ 1.96 × 10³ cm⁻¹**, which is Δ ≈ 19 600 cm⁻¹, or Δ/B ≈ 21.2. This is a value I calculated, not a looked-up one; the main assumptions are listed at the end.\n\n**Approach**\n\nFor octahedral d⁷ there are two candidate ground states:\n- The high-spin ground state is ⁴T₁g(F).\n- The low-spin ground state is ²Eg, which comes mainly from the t₂g⁶eg¹ configuration.\n\nThe crossover is the Dq at which the lowest eigenvalues of these two blocks are equal. I set up both blocks in the strong-field basis. To get the d⁷ matrices, I took the standard Tanabe–Sugano d³ matrices and replaced Dq with −Dq, since d⁷ behaves like three holes. Here C/B = 4156/922 = 4.508.\n\n**⁴T₁g block (2×2)**\n\n| | t₂⁵e² | t₂⁴e³ |\n|---|---|---|\n| t₂⁵e² | −8Dq − 12B | 6B |\n| t₂⁴e³ | 6B | 2Dq − 3B |\n\nThe lower root is E(⁴T₁) = −3Dq − 7.5B − ½√[(10Dq + 9B)² + 144B²].\n\n**²Eg block (4×4)**\n\n| | t₂³e⁴ | t₂⁴e³ (a) | t₂⁴e³ (b) | t₂⁶e¹ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| t₂³e⁴ | 12Dq − 6B + 3C | −6√2 B | −3√2 B | 0 |\n| t₂⁴e³ (a) | −6√2 B | 2Dq + 8B + 6C | 10B | √3(2B + C) |\n| t₂⁴e³ (b) | −3√2 B | 10B | 2Dq − B + 3C | 2√3 B |\n| t₂⁶e¹ | 0 | √3(2B + C) | 2√3 B | −18Dq − 8B + 4C |\n\nThe two t₂⁴e³ rows are the two ²E states of that configuration (from the ¹A₁ and ¹E pairs of t₂ holes). For the ⁴T₁ block, the Dq = 0 eigenvalues reproduce the free-ion ⁴F and ⁴P energies. For the ²E block I only checked that the trace equals the sum of the free-ion ²H, ²G and two ²D energies (−7B + 16C). This trace check matches, but it does not fully prove the off-diagonal elements are right.\n\n**Finding the crossing (energies in units of B; x = Dq/B)**\n\nI found the lowest ²E root by solving the eigenvalue equation numerically (folding the other three states into the t₂⁶e¹ state and iterating to self-consistency).\n\n| x | E(⁴T₁) | E(²E) | E(²E) − E(⁴T₁) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2.10 | −29.96 | −29.72 | +0.23 |\n| 2.20 | −30.72 | −31.47 | −0.75 |\n| 2.124 | −30.139 | −30.142 | ≈ 0 |\n\nThe difference changes sign between 2.10 and 2.20. Interpolating and refining puts the crossing at x ≈ 2.124, so Dq ≈ 2.124 × 922 ≈ 1958 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I wrote down the d³ ²E interaction matrix from memory. The trace check supports it, but I did not check each element against a reference.\n- The answer is sensitive to small errors. Each 0.1 in x shifts Dq by about 90 cm⁻¹, so treat the result as roughly 1960 ± a few tens of cm⁻¹.\n- The model is the pure crystal-field Tanabe–Sugano model: no spin–orbit coupling and no Trees correction.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1958, \"B\": 922, \"C\": 4156, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Crossing of the lowest 4T1g and 2Eg energies, diagonalised in the full Tanabe-Sugano d7 matrices, falls at Dq/B = 2.124.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":793,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":793,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d3c0ea7-d638-42e4-840e-1fac2bf220e9","uuid":"8e841e6c-755c-416d-997e-634582efdeea","timestamp":"2026-11-03T22:08:33.822Z","request_id":"req_011Cggkmdaev6rbxDgSnbxTC"}
